 when sink line cannot be inferred directly, reduce confidence to ≤0.35 and do not assign CWE. Step 3: verify validation at every transition; identify missing intermediate validation specifically for derived pointer/index at sink; confirm unguarded path by quoting guard-absent sink line; do not claim vulnerability without both source and sink quoted separately in ≤3-line pairs; for loop-derived access quote index derivation + loop-access separately with both ranges; specifically check index/offset derivation from untrusted input at loop entry, verify bounds check occurs BEFORE access with quoted snippet pair covering both lines, and state "loop-bound from untrusted count" when applicable; verify termination exits dangerous path with quoted termination line; for derived-pointer loops, insist on separate evidence for derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and guard on derived object — if any missing, treat as unguarded only when sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability. Branch — for each if/else, state condition controlling attacker input. Confirm guard is BEFORE dangerous operation (G before S with quoted G and S). Verify missing break/return/continue allows fallthrough past guard to sink; quote guard and fallthrough path with both line numbers. Check operator correctness (> vs >=, off-by-one, unsigned wrap, underflow/overflow, sign-mismatch) with quoted snippet including line numbers; quote the guard's comparison expression with its line number and the sink's access line separately. Confirm guard applies to sink's accessed object: explicitly state "mismatch: guard checks parent (X) at G but sink dereferences derived pointer (Y) at S" with both line numbers as separate snippet pairs; when derived pointer unvalidated at sink treat path as unguarded rather than relying on parent check; for authorization confirm success/failure assignment cannot be unconditionally overwritten, quote both with ranges; quote each branch sink independently and do not claim branch-controlled vulnerability if sink line is missing; for multi-branch code, quote sink for every branch that reaches a dangerous operation and confirm guard applies to that branch's specific object. Loop — for each for/while state bound (constant/parsed/user/array-length from untrusted). Quote index derivation expression + array/structure access together (≤3 lines including index expression and access/write). Confirm index/offset/count derived from untrusted input; verify bounds check occurs BEFORE access with quoted snippet pair (check line + access line). When loop writes through derived pointer quote derived assignment + loop access together with both ranges; confirm guard applies to derived pointer, not only parent — state "guard applies to parent (P) at G but loop accesses derived pointer (D) at L" if mismatch. Verify termination exits dangerous path; quote termination snippet with line number; verify loop bounds against structure sizes with quoted pairs covering bound expression and access; before loop-based claim confirm both index-derivation and loop-access snippets separately quoted with ranges; if either >3 lines reduce confidence; specifically for index derived from offset/count, quote derivation expression at its assignment line and loop access at its use line as independent pairs. Sink-order analysis (addressing localization/ordering failure): before assigning CWE or confidence, identify the exact dangerous operation type in order of observability — (a) direct memory dereference/write via pointer/index at specific line, (b) authorization/auth decision overwrite/branch bypass, (c) arithmetic/integer operation at sink, (d) format/string operation at sink. Quote the sink snippet containing the exact write/dereference/assignment/format line; if sink line must be inferred rather than directly quoted, reduce confidence to ≤0.35 and do not assign CWE; do not claim memory vulnerability from format-string absence alone. For derived-pointer loops: quote derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and any guard on derived pointer separately; do not merge into a single multi-line snippet. Evidence anchoring (addressing sparse/low-specificity evidence): for every vulnerability claim include at least three evidence items — one for source/derived-pointer assignment (with range), one for sink (with exact range containing dangerous operation), and one for validation/guard state at transition (with both guard and sink line when relevant); all must be ≤3 lines and independently quoted; include derived-pointer assignment + sink pair with both ranges when derived pointer involved; include guard-object mismatch statement as separate evidence when applicable; state whether guard applies to attacker-controlled path; include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; prefer narrow ranges (≤3) for high confidence, wider only unavoidable with stated reduction; when derived-pointer validity at sink unquoted, include explicit limitation note rather than using absence as evidence. CWE identification (select only from observable sink type, addressing misidentification from inference): memory dereference/access = CWE-787/125/416; input validation failure at sink = CWE-20; authorization/auth bypass = CWE-287/862; arithmetic/integer at sink = CWE-190/191; format/string = CWE-134/78; select most specific directly observable from quoted sink snippet, not from validation behavior or absence alone; prefer observable sink over inference; list at most one CWE unless multiple clearly distinct sink types in different branches visible; do NOT list CWE when only source is quoted and sink is missing/inferred, when only guard absence is noted without quoted sink, when sink is format/string but format string is literal (attacker controls only data argument), or when only validation behavior is described; if no dangerous sink quoted, omit CWE entirely; when sink is memory write through derived pointer, prefer CWE-787; never assign CWE based solely on unquoted derived-pointer validity or guard absence. Localization (addressing low loc-f1): snippet must contain sink line(s); include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; use ≤3 line ranges; include function name; if function/file unknown state null; for multi-branch cases quote each branch sink independently; when derived pointer at sink, quote the exact dereference line (not the parent assignment); include start_line and end_line that tightly bound the dangerous operation; never use full-function ranges for sink evidence. Conservative verification (addressing over-labeling/precision failure — high FP from missing sink quotes, guard-object mismatches, unquoted derived-pointer validity): only claim vulnerability when both source and sink visible as quoted pairs, unguarded or guard misplaced/applying to wrong object, with specific line ranges; base reasoning only on given code; follow actual control flow; when guard checks parent but sink uses derived pointer state mismatch explicitly with both line numbers and treat derived-pointer path as unguarded — do not claim vulnerability solely on parent check; if ambiguous (snippet >3 lines, guard object differs from sink object, missing source/sink pair, full-function range, derived-pointer validity at sink never quoted, sink inferred not quoted, no CWE-assignable sink visible, loop index derivation unquoted, derived pointer assignment missing) prefer false over true and reduce confidence; set confidence proportional to evidence specificity — narrow range + snippet at both source and sink + correct guard-check + quoted derived-pointer validity = high (≥0.8); narrow range + source/sink quoted + guard mismatch stated = moderate-high (0.6–0.75); inference/full-range/guard-mismatch/missing sink quote/unquoted derived validity/loop index unquoted = moderate (0.4–0.7); missing source or missing sink or inferred sink or no quoted dangerous operation or literal format with data-only control = low/false (≤0.35); emphasize absence of quoted derived-pointer validity check at sink is limitation not evidence; avoid inferring vulnerability from absence of guard alone when sink not quoted; require both source and sink quoted separately for every claim; when derived pointer access at sink not validated by quoted snippet at sink line treat as unguarded only if sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability; specifically avoid assigning CWE or high confidence when only source quoted and sink missing/inferred, when format string is literal, or when sink inferred rather than quoted; when loop index derived from untrusted input and access line not independently quoted with range, reduce confidence proportionally; do not assign CWE-134/78 when attacker controls only format argument, not the format specification itself. Format: first SCoT reasoning with numbered Sequence/Branch/Loop steps using quoted snippet pairs with line ranges (start_line/end_line) and explicit mismatch/guard statements, ordered sink-type identification, then ONLY one JSON object with no markdown/extra text. JSON: {"verdict":{"vulnerable":true/false,"confidence":0.0-1.0,"cwe":["CWE-..."],"severity":"low|medium|high|critical|unknown","summary":"..."},"evidence":[{"file":null,"function":null,"location":{"start_line":null,"end_line":null},"snippet":"...","reason":"..."}],"notes":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":[]}} Code: void pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rx_rtcp_xr( pjmedia_rtcp_xr_session *sess,
				 const void *pkt,
				 pj_size_t size)
{
    const pjmedia_rtcp_xr_pkt	      *rtcp_xr = (pjmedia_rtcp_xr_pkt*) pkt;
    const pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_rr_time  *rb_rr_time = NULL;
    const pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_dlrr     *rb_dlrr = NULL;
    const pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_stats    *rb_stats = NULL;
    const pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_voip_mtc *rb_voip_mtc = NULL;
    const pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_header   *rb_hdr = (pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_header*) 
						rtcp_xr->buf;
    unsigned pkt_len, rb_len;

    if (rtcp_xr->common.pt != RTCP_XR)
	return;

    pkt_len = pj_ntohs((pj_uint16_t)rtcp_xr->common.length);

    if ((pkt_len + 1) > (size / 4))
	return;

    /* Parse report rpt_types */
    while ((pj_int32_t*)rb_hdr < (pj_int32_t*)pkt + pkt_len)
    {	
	rb_len = pj_ntohs((pj_uint16_t)rb_hdr->length);

	/* Just skip any block with length == 0 (no report content) */
	if (rb_len) {
	    switch (rb_hdr->bt) {
		case BT_RR_TIME:
		    rb_rr_time = (pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_rr_time*) rb_hdr;
		    break;
		case BT_DLRR:
		    rb_dlrr = (pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_dlrr*) rb_hdr;
		    break;
		case BT_STATS:
		    rb_stats = (pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_stats*) rb_hdr;
		    break;
		case BT_VOIP_METRICS:
		    rb_voip_mtc = (pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_voip_mtc*) rb_hdr;
		    break;
		default:
		    break;
	    }
	}
	rb_hdr = (pjmedia_rtcp_xr_rb_header*)
		 ((pj_int32_t*)rb_hdr + rb_len + 1);
    }

    /* Receiving RR Time */
    if (rb_rr_time) {
	/* Save LRR from NTP timestamp of the RR time block report */
	sess->rx_lrr = ((pj_ntohl(rb_rr_time->ntp_sec) & 0x0000FFFF) << 16) | 
		       ((pj_ntohl(rb_rr_time->ntp_frac) >> 16) & 0xFFFF);

	/* Calculate RR arrival time for DLRR */
	pj_get_timestamp(&sess->rx_lrr_time);

	TRACE_((sess->name, "Rx RTCP SR: ntp_ts=%p", sess->rx_lrr,
	       (pj_uint32_t)(sess->rx_lrr_time.u64*65536/
			     sess->rtcp_session->ts_freq.u64)));
    }

    /* Receiving DLRR */
    if (rb_dlrr) {
	pj_uint32_t lrr, now, dlrr;
	pj_uint64_t eedelay;
	pjmedia_rtcp_ntp_rec ntp;

	/* LRR is the middle 32bit of NTP. It has 1/65536 second 
	 * resolution 
	 */
	lrr = pj_ntohl(rb_dlrr->item.lrr);

	/* DLRR is delay since LRR, also in 1/65536 resolution */
	dlrr = pj_ntohl(rb_dlrr->item.dlrr);

	/* Get current time, and convert to 1/65536 resolution */
	pjmedia_rtcp_get_ntp_time(sess->rtcp_session, &ntp);
	now = ((ntp.hi & 0xFFFF) << 16) + (ntp.lo >> 16);

	/* End-to-end delay is (now-lrr-dlrr) */
	eedelay = now - lrr - dlrr;

	/* Convert end to end delay to usec (keeping the calculation in
         * 64bit space)::
	 *   sess->ee_delay = (eedelay * 1000) / 65536;
	 */
	if (eedelay < 4294) {
	    eedelay = (eedelay * 1000000) >> 16;
	} else {
	    eedelay = (eedelay * 1000) >> 16;
	    eedelay *= 1000;
	}

	TRACE_((sess->name, "Rx RTCP XR DLRR: lrr=%p, dlrr=%p (%d:%03dms), "
			   "now=%p, rtt=%p",
		lrr, dlrr, dlrr/65536, (dlrr%65536)*1000/65536,
		now, (pj_uint32_t)eedelay));

	/* Only save calculation if "now" is greater than lrr, or
	 * otherwise rtt will be invalid 
	 */
	if (now-dlrr >= lrr) {
	    unsigned rtt = (pj_uint32_t)eedelay;

	    /* Check that eedelay value really makes sense. 
	     * We allow up to 30 seconds RTT!
	     */
	    if (eedelay <= 30 * 1000 * 1000UL) {
		/* "Normalize" rtt value that is exceptionally high.
		 * For such values, "normalize" the rtt to be three times
		 * the average value.
		 */
		if (rtt>((unsigned)sess->stat.rtt.mean*3) && sess->stat.rtt.n!=0)
		{
		    unsigned orig_rtt = rtt;
		    rtt = (unsigned)sess->stat.rtt.mean*3;
		    PJ_LOG(5,(sess->name, 
			      "RTT value %d usec is normalized to %d usec",
			      orig_rtt, rtt));
		}

		TRACE_((sess->name, "RTCP RTT is set to %d usec", rtt));
		pj_math_stat_update(&sess->stat.rtt, rtt);
	    }
	} else {
	    PJ_LOG(5, (sess->name, "Internal RTCP NTP clock skew detected: "
				   "lrr=%p, now=%p, dlrr=%p (%d:%03dms), "
				   "diff=%d",
				   lrr, now, dlrr, dlrr/65536,
				   (dlrr%65536)*1000/65536,
				   dlrr-(now-lrr)));
	}
    }

    /* Receiving Statistics Summary */
    if (rb_stats) {
	pj_uint8_t flags = rb_stats->header.specific;

	pj_bzero(&sess->stat.tx.stat_sum, sizeof(sess->stat.tx.stat_sum));

	/* Range of packets sequence reported in this blocks */
	sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.begin_seq = pj_ntohs(rb_stats->begin_seq);
	sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.end_seq   = pj_ntohs(rb_stats->end_seq);

	/* Get flags of valid fields */
	sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.l = (flags & (1 << 7)) != 0;
	sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.d = (flags & (1 << 6)) != 0;
	sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.j = (flags & (1 << 5)) != 0;
	sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.t = (flags & (3 << 3)) != 0;

	/* Fetch the reports info */
	if (sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.l) {
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.lost = pj_ntohl(rb_stats->lost);
	}

	if (sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.d) {
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.dup = pj_ntohl(rb_stats->dup);
	}

	if (sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.j) {
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.jitter.min = pj_ntohl(rb_stats->jitter_min);
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.jitter.max = pj_ntohl(rb_stats->jitter_max);
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.jitter.mean= pj_ntohl(rb_stats->jitter_mean);
	    pj_math_stat_set_stddev(&sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.jitter, 
				    pj_ntohl(rb_stats->jitter_dev));
	}

	if (sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.t) {
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.toh.min = rb_stats->toh_min;
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.toh.max = rb_stats->toh_max;
	    sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.toh.mean= rb_stats->toh_mean;
	    pj_math_stat_set_stddev(&sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.toh, 
				    pj_ntohl(rb_stats->toh_dev));
	}

	pj_gettimeofday(&sess->stat.tx.stat_sum.update);
    }

    /* Receiving VoIP Metrics */
    if (rb_voip_mtc) {
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.loss_rate = rb_voip_mtc->loss_rate;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.discard_rate = rb_voip_mtc->discard_rate;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.burst_den = rb_voip_mtc->burst_den;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.gap_den = rb_voip_mtc->gap_den;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.burst_dur = pj_ntohs(rb_voip_mtc->burst_dur);
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.gap_dur = pj_ntohs(rb_voip_mtc->gap_dur);
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.rnd_trip_delay = 
					pj_ntohs(rb_voip_mtc->rnd_trip_delay);
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.end_sys_delay = 
					pj_ntohs(rb_voip_mtc->end_sys_delay);
	/* signal & noise level encoded in two's complement form */
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.signal_lvl = (pj_int8_t)
				    ((rb_voip_mtc->signal_lvl > 127)?
				     ((int)rb_voip_mtc->signal_lvl - 256) : 
				     rb_voip_mtc->signal_lvl);
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.noise_lvl  = (pj_int8_t)
				    ((rb_voip_mtc->noise_lvl > 127)?
				     ((int)rb_voip_mtc->noise_lvl - 256) : 
				     rb_voip_mtc->noise_lvl);
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.rerl = rb_voip_mtc->rerl;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.gmin = rb_voip_mtc->gmin;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.r_factor = rb_voip_mtc->r_factor;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.ext_r_factor = rb_voip_mtc->ext_r_factor;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.mos_lq = rb_voip_mtc->mos_lq;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.mos_cq = rb_voip_mtc->mos_cq;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.rx_config = rb_voip_mtc->rx_config;
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.jb_nom = pj_ntohs(rb_voip_mtc->jb_nom);
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.jb_max = pj_ntohs(rb_voip_mtc->jb_max);
	sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.jb_abs_max = pj_ntohs(rb_voip_mtc->jb_abs_max);

	pj_gettimeofday(&sess->stat.tx.voip_mtc.update);
    }
}

