quote derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and any guard on derived pointer separately; do not merge into a single multi-line snippet. Evidence anchoring (addressing sparse/low-specificity evidence): for every vulnerability claim include at least three evidence items — one for source/derived-pointer assignment (with range), one for sink (with exact range containing dangerous operation), and one for validation/guard state at transition (with both guard and sink line when relevant); all must be ≤3 lines and independently quoted; include derived-pointer assignment + sink pair with both ranges when derived pointer involved; include guard-object mismatch statement as separate evidence when applicable; state whether guard applies to attacker-controlled path; include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; prefer narrow ranges (≤3) for high confidence, wider only unavoidable with stated reduction; when derived-pointer validity at sink unquoted, include explicit limitation note rather than using absence as evidence. CWE identification (select only from observable sink type, addressing misidentification from inference): memory dereference/access = CWE-787/125/416; input validation failure at sink = CWE-20; authorization/auth bypass = CWE-287/862; arithmetic/integer at sink = CWE-190/191; format/string = CWE-134/78; select most specific directly observable from quoted sink snippet, not from validation behavior or absence alone; prefer observable sink over inference; list at most one CWE unless multiple clearly distinct sink types in different branches visible; do NOT list CWE when only source is quoted and sink is missing/inferred, when only guard absence is noted without quoted sink, when sink is format/string but format string is literal (attacker controls only data argument), or when only validation behavior is described; if no dangerous sink quoted, omit CWE entirely; when sink is memory write through derived pointer, prefer CWE-787; never assign CWE based solely on unquoted derived-pointer validity or guard absence. Localization (addressing low loc-f1): snippet must contain sink line(s); include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; use ≤3 line ranges; include function name; if function/file unknown state null; for multi-branch cases quote each branch sink independently; when derived pointer at sink, quote the exact dereference line (not the parent assignment); include start_line and end_line that tightly bound the dangerous operation; never use full-function ranges for sink evidence. Conservative verification (addressing over-labeling/precision failure — high FP from missing sink quotes, guard-object mismatches, unquoted derived-pointer validity): only claim vulnerability when both source and sink visible as quoted pairs, unguarded or guard misplaced/applying to wrong object, with specific line ranges; base reasoning only on given code; follow actual control flow; when guard checks parent but sink uses derived pointer state mismatch explicitly with both line numbers and treat derived-pointer path as unguarded — do not claim vulnerability solely on parent check; if ambiguous (snippet >3 lines, guard object differs from sink object, missing source/sink pair, full-function range, derived-pointer validity at sink never quoted, sink inferred not quoted, no CWE-assignable sink visible, loop index derivation unquoted, derived pointer assignment missing) prefer false over true and reduce confidence; set confidence proportional to evidence specificity — narrow range + snippet at both source and sink + correct guard-check + quoted derived-pointer validity = high (≥0.8); narrow range + source/sink quoted + guard mismatch stated = moderate-high (0.6–0.75); inference/full-range/guard-mismatch/missing sink quote/unquoted derived validity/loop index unquoted = moderate (0.4–0.7); missing source or missing sink or inferred sink or no quoted dangerous operation or literal format with data-only control = low/false (≤0.35); emphasize absence of quoted derived-pointer validity check at sink is limitation not evidence; avoid inferring vulnerability from absence of guard alone when sink not quoted; require both source and sink quoted separately for every claim; when derived pointer access at sink not validated by quoted snippet at sink line treat as unguarded only if sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability; specifically avoid assigning CWE or high confidence when only source quoted and sink missing/inferred, when format string is literal, or when sink inferred rather than quoted; when loop index derived from untrusted input and access line not independently quoted with range, reduce confidence proportionally; do not assign CWE-134/78 when attacker controls only format argument, not the format specification itself. Format: first SCoT reasoning with numbered Sequence/Branch/Loop steps using quoted snippet pairs with line ranges (start_line/end_line) and explicit mismatch/guard statements, ordered sink-type identification, then ONLY one JSON object with no markdown/extra text. JSON: {"verdict":{"vulnerable":true/false,"confidence":0.0-1.0,"cwe":["CWE-..."],"severity":"low|medium|high|critical|unknown","summary":"..."},"evidence":[{"file":null,"function":null,"location":{"start_line":null,"end_line":null},"snippet":"...","reason":"..."}],"notes":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":[]}} Code: get_one_sourceline(source_cookie_T *sp)
{
    garray_T		ga;
    int			len;
    int			c;
    char_u		*buf;
#ifdef USE_CRNL
    int			has_cr;		// CR-LF found
#endif
    int			have_read = FALSE;

    // use a growarray to store the sourced line
    ga_init2(&ga, 1, 250);

    // Loop until there is a finished line (or end-of-file).
    ++sp->sourcing_lnum;
    for (;;)
    {
	// make room to read at least 120 (more) characters
	if (ga_grow(&ga, 120) == FAIL)
	    break;
	if (sp->source_from_buf)
	{
	    if (sp->buf_lnum >= sp->buflines.ga_len)
		break;		    // all the lines are processed
	    ga_concat(&ga, ((char_u **)sp->buflines.ga_data)[sp->buf_lnum]);
	    sp->buf_lnum++;
	    if (ga_grow(&ga, 1) == FAIL)
		break;
	    buf = (char_u *)ga.ga_data;
	    buf[ga.ga_len++] = NUL;
	    len = ga.ga_len;
	}
	else
	{
	    buf = (char_u *)ga.ga_data;
	    if (fgets((char *)buf + ga.ga_len, ga.ga_maxlen - ga.ga_len,
			sp->fp) == NULL)
		break;
	    len = ga.ga_len + (int)STRLEN(buf + ga.ga_len);
	}
#ifdef USE_CRNL
	// Ignore a trailing CTRL-Z, when in Dos mode.	Only recognize the
	// CTRL-Z by its own, or after a NL.
	if (	   (len == 1 || (len >= 2 && buf[len - 2] == '\n'))
		&& sp->fileformat == EOL_DOS
		&& buf[len - 1] == Ctrl_Z)
	{
	    buf[len - 1] = NUL;
	    break;
	}
#endif

	have_read = TRUE;
	ga.ga_len = len;

	// If the line was longer than the buffer, read more.
	if (ga.ga_maxlen - ga.ga_len == 1 && buf[len - 1] != '\n')
	    continue;

	if (len >= 1 && buf[len - 1] == '\n')	// remove trailing NL
	{
#ifdef USE_CRNL
	    has_cr = (len >= 2 && buf[len - 2] == '\r');
	    if (sp->fileformat == EOL_UNKNOWN)
	    {
		if (has_cr)
		    sp->fileformat = EOL_DOS;
		else
		    sp->fileformat = EOL_UNIX;
	    }

	    if (sp->fileformat == EOL_DOS)
	    {
		if (has_cr)	    // replace trailing CR
		{
		    buf[len - 2] = '\n';
		    --len;
		    --ga.ga_len;
		}
		else	    // lines like ":map xx yy^M" will have failed
		{
		    if (!sp->error)
		    {
			msg_source(HL_ATTR(HLF_W));
			emsg(_("W15: Warning: Wrong line separator, ^M may be missing"));
		    }
		    sp->error = TRUE;
		    sp->fileformat = EOL_UNIX;
		}
	    }
#endif
	    // The '\n' is escaped if there is an odd number of ^V's just
	    // before it, first set "c" just before the 'V's and then check
	    // len&c parities (is faster than ((len-c)%2 == 0)) -- Acevedo
	    for (c = len - 2; c >= 0 && buf[c] == Ctrl_V; c--)
		;
	    if ((len & 1) != (c & 1))	// escaped NL, read more
	    {
		++sp->sourcing_lnum;
		continue;
	    }

	    buf[len - 1] = NUL;		// remove the NL
	}

	// Check for ^C here now and then, so recursive :so can be broken.
	line_breakcheck();
	break;
    }

    if (have_read)
	return (char_u *)ga.ga_data;

    vim_free(ga.ga_data);
    return NULL;
}

