 when sink line cannot be inferred directly, reduce confidence to ≤0.35 and do not assign CWE. Step 3: verify validation at every transition; identify missing intermediate validation specifically for derived pointer/index at sink; confirm unguarded path by quoting guard-absent sink line; do not claim vulnerability without both source and sink quoted separately in ≤3-line pairs; for loop-derived access quote index derivation + loop-access separately with both ranges; specifically check index/offset derivation from untrusted input at loop entry, verify bounds check occurs BEFORE access with quoted snippet pair covering both lines, and state "loop-bound from untrusted count" when applicable; verify termination exits dangerous path with quoted termination line; for derived-pointer loops, insist on separate evidence for derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and guard on derived object — if any missing, treat as unguarded only when sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability. Branch — for each if/else, state condition controlling attacker input. Confirm guard is BEFORE dangerous operation (G before S with quoted G and S). Verify missing break/return/continue allows fallthrough past guard to sink; quote guard and fallthrough path with both line numbers. Check operator correctness (> vs >=, off-by-one, unsigned wrap, underflow/overflow, sign-mismatch) with quoted snippet including line numbers; quote the guard's comparison expression with its line number and the sink's access line separately. Confirm guard applies to sink's accessed object: explicitly state "mismatch: guard checks parent (X) at G but sink dereferences derived pointer (Y) at S" with both line numbers as separate snippet pairs; when derived pointer unvalidated at sink treat path as unguarded rather than relying on parent check; for authorization confirm success/failure assignment cannot be unconditionally overwritten, quote both with ranges; quote each branch sink independently and do not claim branch-controlled vulnerability if sink line is missing; for multi-branch code, quote sink for every branch that reaches a dangerous operation and confirm guard applies to that branch's specific object. Loop — for each for/while state bound (constant/parsed/user/array-length from untrusted). Quote index derivation expression + array/structure access together (≤3 lines including index expression and access/write). Confirm index/offset/count derived from untrusted input; verify bounds check occurs BEFORE access with quoted snippet pair (check line + access line). When loop writes through derived pointer quote derived assignment + loop access together with both ranges; confirm guard applies to derived pointer, not only parent — state "guard applies to parent (P) at G but loop accesses derived pointer (D) at L" if mismatch. Verify termination exits dangerous path; quote termination snippet with line number; verify loop bounds against structure sizes with quoted pairs covering bound expression and access; before loop-based claim confirm both index-derivation and loop-access snippets separately quoted with ranges; if either >3 lines reduce confidence; specifically for index derived from offset/count, quote derivation expression at its assignment line and loop access at its use line as independent pairs. Sink-order analysis (addressing localization/ordering failure): before assigning CWE or confidence, identify the exact dangerous operation type in order of observability — (a) direct memory dereference/write via pointer/index at specific line, (b) authorization/auth decision overwrite/branch bypass, (c) arithmetic/integer operation at sink, (d) format/string operation at sink. Quote the sink snippet containing the exact write/dereference/assignment/format line; if sink line must be inferred rather than directly quoted, reduce confidence to ≤0.35 and do not assign CWE; do not claim memory vulnerability from format-string absence alone. For derived-pointer loops: quote derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and any guard on derived pointer separately; do not merge into a single multi-line snippet. Evidence anchoring (addressing sparse/low-specificity evidence): for every vulnerability claim include at least three evidence items — one for source/derived-pointer assignment (with range), one for sink (with exact range containing dangerous operation), and one for validation/guard state at transition (with both guard and sink line when relevant); all must be ≤3 lines and independently quoted; include derived-pointer assignment + sink pair with both ranges when derived pointer involved; include guard-object mismatch statement as separate evidence when applicable; state whether guard applies to attacker-controlled path; include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; prefer narrow ranges (≤3) for high confidence, wider only unavoidable with stated reduction; when derived-pointer validity at sink unquoted, include explicit limitation note rather than using absence as evidence. CWE identification (select only from observable sink type, addressing misidentification from inference): memory dereference/access = CWE-787/125/416; input validation failure at sink = CWE-20; authorization/auth bypass = CWE-287/862; arithmetic/integer at sink = CWE-190/191; format/string = CWE-134/78; select most specific directly observable from quoted sink snippet, not from validation behavior or absence alone; prefer observable sink over inference; list at most one CWE unless multiple clearly distinct sink types in different branches visible; do NOT list CWE when only source is quoted and sink is missing/inferred, when only guard absence is noted without quoted sink, when sink is format/string but format string is literal (attacker controls only data argument), or when only validation behavior is described; if no dangerous sink quoted, omit CWE entirely; when sink is memory write through derived pointer, prefer CWE-787; never assign CWE based solely on unquoted derived-pointer validity or guard absence. Localization (addressing low loc-f1): snippet must contain sink line(s); include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; use ≤3 line ranges; include function name; if function/file unknown state null; for multi-branch cases quote each branch sink independently; when derived pointer at sink, quote the exact dereference line (not the parent assignment); include start_line and end_line that tightly bound the dangerous operation; never use full-function ranges for sink evidence. Conservative verification (addressing over-labeling/precision failure — high FP from missing sink quotes, guard-object mismatches, unquoted derived-pointer validity): only claim vulnerability when both source and sink visible as quoted pairs, unguarded or guard misplaced/applying to wrong object, with specific line ranges; base reasoning only on given code; follow actual control flow; when guard checks parent but sink uses derived pointer state mismatch explicitly with both line numbers and treat derived-pointer path as unguarded — do not claim vulnerability solely on parent check; if ambiguous (snippet >3 lines, guard object differs from sink object, missing source/sink pair, full-function range, derived-pointer validity at sink never quoted, sink inferred not quoted, no CWE-assignable sink visible, loop index derivation unquoted, derived pointer assignment missing) prefer false over true and reduce confidence; set confidence proportional to evidence specificity — narrow range + snippet at both source and sink + correct guard-check + quoted derived-pointer validity = high (≥0.8); narrow range + source/sink quoted + guard mismatch stated = moderate-high (0.6–0.75); inference/full-range/guard-mismatch/missing sink quote/unquoted derived validity/loop index unquoted = moderate (0.4–0.7); missing source or missing sink or inferred sink or no quoted dangerous operation or literal format with data-only control = low/false (≤0.35); emphasize absence of quoted derived-pointer validity check at sink is limitation not evidence; avoid inferring vulnerability from absence of guard alone when sink not quoted; require both source and sink quoted separately for every claim; when derived pointer access at sink not validated by quoted snippet at sink line treat as unguarded only if sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability; specifically avoid assigning CWE or high confidence when only source quoted and sink missing/inferred, when format string is literal, or when sink inferred rather than quoted; when loop index derived from untrusted input and access line not independently quoted with range, reduce confidence proportionally; do not assign CWE-134/78 when attacker controls only format argument, not the format specification itself. Format: first SCoT reasoning with numbered Sequence/Branch/Loop steps using quoted snippet pairs with line ranges (start_line/end_line) and explicit mismatch/guard statements, ordered sink-type identification, then ONLY one JSON object with no markdown/extra text. JSON: {"verdict":{"vulnerable":true/false,"confidence":0.0-1.0,"cwe":["CWE-..."],"severity":"low|medium|high|critical|unknown","summary":"..."},"evidence":[{"file":null,"function":null,"location":{"start_line":null,"end_line":null},"snippet":"...","reason":"..."}],"notes":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":[]}} Code: static int nft_verdict_init(const struct nft_ctx *ctx, struct nft_data *data,
			    struct nft_data_desc *desc, const struct nlattr *nla)
{
	u8 genmask = nft_genmask_next(ctx->net);
	struct nlattr *tb[NFTA_VERDICT_MAX + 1];
	struct nft_chain *chain;
	int err;

	err = nla_parse_nested_deprecated(tb, NFTA_VERDICT_MAX, nla,
					  nft_verdict_policy, NULL);
	if (err < 0)
		return err;

	if (!tb[NFTA_VERDICT_CODE])
		return -EINVAL;
	data->verdict.code = ntohl(nla_get_be32(tb[NFTA_VERDICT_CODE]));

	switch (data->verdict.code) {
	default:
		switch (data->verdict.code & NF_VERDICT_MASK) {
		case NF_ACCEPT:
		case NF_DROP:
		case NF_QUEUE:
			break;
		default:
			return -EINVAL;
		}
		fallthrough;
	case NFT_CONTINUE:
	case NFT_BREAK:
	case NFT_RETURN:
		break;
	case NFT_JUMP:
	case NFT_GOTO:
		if (tb[NFTA_VERDICT_CHAIN]) {
			chain = nft_chain_lookup(ctx->net, ctx->table,
						 tb[NFTA_VERDICT_CHAIN],
						 genmask);
		} else if (tb[NFTA_VERDICT_CHAIN_ID]) {
			chain = nft_chain_lookup_byid(ctx->net, ctx->table,
						      tb[NFTA_VERDICT_CHAIN_ID]);
			if (IS_ERR(chain))
				return PTR_ERR(chain);
		} else {
			return -EINVAL;
		}

		if (IS_ERR(chain))
			return PTR_ERR(chain);
		if (nft_is_base_chain(chain))
			return -EOPNOTSUPP;
		if (nft_chain_is_bound(chain))
			return -EINVAL;
		if (desc->flags & NFT_DATA_DESC_SETELEM &&
		    chain->flags & NFT_CHAIN_BINDING)
			return -EINVAL;

		chain->use++;
		data->verdict.chain = chain;
		break;
	}

	desc->len = sizeof(data->verdict);

	return 0;
}

