 when sink line cannot be inferred directly, reduce confidence to ≤0.35 and do not assign CWE. Step 3: verify validation at every transition; identify missing intermediate validation specifically for derived pointer/index at sink; confirm unguarded path by quoting guard-absent sink line; do not claim vulnerability without both source and sink quoted separately in ≤3-line pairs; for loop-derived access quote index derivation + loop-access separately with both ranges; specifically check index/offset derivation from untrusted input at loop entry, verify bounds check occurs BEFORE access with quoted snippet pair covering both lines, and state "loop-bound from untrusted count" when applicable; verify termination exits dangerous path with quoted termination line; for derived-pointer loops, insist on separate evidence for derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and guard on derived object — if any missing, treat as unguarded only when sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability. Branch — for each if/else, state condition controlling attacker input. Confirm guard is BEFORE dangerous operation (G before S with quoted G and S). Verify missing break/return/continue allows fallthrough past guard to sink; quote guard and fallthrough path with both line numbers. Check operator correctness (> vs >=, off-by-one, unsigned wrap, underflow/overflow, sign-mismatch) with quoted snippet including line numbers; quote the guard's comparison expression with its line number and the sink's access line separately. Confirm guard applies to sink's accessed object: explicitly state "mismatch: guard checks parent (X) at G but sink dereferences derived pointer (Y) at S" with both line numbers as separate snippet pairs; when derived pointer unvalidated at sink treat path as unguarded rather than relying on parent check; for authorization confirm success/failure assignment cannot be unconditionally overwritten, quote both with ranges; quote each branch sink independently and do not claim branch-controlled vulnerability if sink line is missing; for multi-branch code, quote sink for every branch that reaches a dangerous operation and confirm guard applies to that branch's specific object. Loop — for each for/while state bound (constant/parsed/user/array-length from untrusted). Quote index derivation expression + array/structure access together (≤3 lines including index expression and access/write). Confirm index/offset/count derived from untrusted input; verify bounds check occurs BEFORE access with quoted snippet pair (check line + access line). When loop writes through derived pointer quote derived assignment + loop access together with both ranges; confirm guard applies to derived pointer, not only parent — state "guard applies to parent (P) at G but loop accesses derived pointer (D) at L" if mismatch. Verify termination exits dangerous path; quote termination snippet with line number; verify loop bounds against structure sizes with quoted pairs covering bound expression and access; before loop-based claim confirm both index-derivation and loop-access snippets separately quoted with ranges; if either >3 lines reduce confidence; specifically for index derived from offset/count, quote derivation expression at its assignment line and loop access at its use line as independent pairs. Sink-order analysis (addressing localization/ordering failure): before assigning CWE or confidence, identify the exact dangerous operation type in order of observability — (a) direct memory dereference/write via pointer/index at specific line, (b) authorization/auth decision overwrite/branch bypass, (c) arithmetic/integer operation at sink, (d) format/string operation at sink. Quote the sink snippet containing the exact write/dereference/assignment/format line; if sink line must be inferred rather than directly quoted, reduce confidence to ≤0.35 and do not assign CWE; do not claim memory vulnerability from format-string absence alone. For derived-pointer loops: quote derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and any guard on derived pointer separately; do not merge into a single multi-line snippet. Evidence anchoring (addressing sparse/low-specificity evidence): for every vulnerability claim include at least three evidence items — one for source/derived-pointer assignment (with range), one for sink (with exact range containing dangerous operation), and one for validation/guard state at transition (with both guard and sink line when relevant); all must be ≤3 lines and independently quoted; include derived-pointer assignment + sink pair with both ranges when derived pointer involved; include guard-object mismatch statement as separate evidence when applicable; state whether guard applies to attacker-controlled path; include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; prefer narrow ranges (≤3) for high confidence, wider only unavoidable with stated reduction; when derived-pointer validity at sink unquoted, include explicit limitation note rather than using absence as evidence. CWE identification (select only from observable sink type, addressing misidentification from inference): memory dereference/access = CWE-787/125/416; input validation failure at sink = CWE-20; authorization/auth bypass = CWE-287/862; arithmetic/integer at sink = CWE-190/191; format/string = CWE-134/78; select most specific directly observable from quoted sink snippet, not from validation behavior or absence alone; prefer observable sink over inference; list at most one CWE unless multiple clearly distinct sink types in different branches visible; do NOT list CWE when only source is quoted and sink is missing/inferred, when only guard absence is noted without quoted sink, when sink is format/string but format string is literal (attacker controls only data argument), or when only validation behavior is described; if no dangerous sink quoted, omit CWE entirely; when sink is memory write through derived pointer, prefer CWE-787; never assign CWE based solely on unquoted derived-pointer validity or guard absence. Localization (addressing low loc-f1): snippet must contain sink line(s); include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; use ≤3 line ranges; include function name; if function/file unknown state null; for multi-branch cases quote each branch sink independently; when derived pointer at sink, quote the exact dereference line (not the parent assignment); include start_line and end_line that tightly bound the dangerous operation; never use full-function ranges for sink evidence. Conservative verification (addressing over-labeling/precision failure — high FP from missing sink quotes, guard-object mismatches, unquoted derived-pointer validity): only claim vulnerability when both source and sink visible as quoted pairs, unguarded or guard misplaced/applying to wrong object, with specific line ranges; base reasoning only on given code; follow actual control flow; when guard checks parent but sink uses derived pointer state mismatch explicitly with both line numbers and treat derived-pointer path as unguarded — do not claim vulnerability solely on parent check; if ambiguous (snippet >3 lines, guard object differs from sink object, missing source/sink pair, full-function range, derived-pointer validity at sink never quoted, sink inferred not quoted, no CWE-assignable sink visible, loop index derivation unquoted, derived pointer assignment missing) prefer false over true and reduce confidence; set confidence proportional to evidence specificity — narrow range + snippet at both source and sink + correct guard-check + quoted derived-pointer validity = high (≥0.8); narrow range + source/sink quoted + guard mismatch stated = moderate-high (0.6–0.75); inference/full-range/guard-mismatch/missing sink quote/unquoted derived validity/loop index unquoted = moderate (0.4–0.7); missing source or missing sink or inferred sink or no quoted dangerous operation or literal format with data-only control = low/false (≤0.35); emphasize absence of quoted derived-pointer validity check at sink is limitation not evidence; avoid inferring vulnerability from absence of guard alone when sink not quoted; require both source and sink quoted separately for every claim; when derived pointer access at sink not validated by quoted snippet at sink line treat as unguarded only if sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability; specifically avoid assigning CWE or high confidence when only source quoted and sink missing/inferred, when format string is literal, or when sink inferred rather than quoted; when loop index derived from untrusted input and access line not independently quoted with range, reduce confidence proportionally; do not assign CWE-134/78 when attacker controls only format argument, not the format specification itself. Format: first SCoT reasoning with numbered Sequence/Branch/Loop steps using quoted snippet pairs with line ranges (start_line/end_line) and explicit mismatch/guard statements, ordered sink-type identification, then ONLY one JSON object with no markdown/extra text. JSON: {"verdict":{"vulnerable":true/false,"confidence":0.0-1.0,"cwe":["CWE-..."],"severity":"low|medium|high|critical|unknown","summary":"..."},"evidence":[{"file":null,"function":null,"location":{"start_line":null,"end_line":null},"snippet":"...","reason":"..."}],"notes":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":[]}} Code: do_arg_all(
    int	count,
    int	forceit,		// hide buffers in current windows
    int keep_tabs)		// keep current tabs, for ":tab drop file"
{
    int		i;
    win_T	*wp, *wpnext;
    char_u	*opened;	// Array of weight for which args are open:
				//  0: not opened
				//  1: opened in other tab
				//  2: opened in curtab
				//  3: opened in curtab and curwin
				//
    int		opened_len;	// length of opened[]
    int		use_firstwin = FALSE;	// use first window for arglist
    int		tab_drop_empty_window = FALSE;
    int		split_ret = OK;
    int		p_ea_save;
    alist_T	*alist;		// argument list to be used
    buf_T	*buf;
    tabpage_T	*tpnext;
    int		had_tab = cmdmod.cmod_tab;
    win_T	*old_curwin, *last_curwin;
    tabpage_T	*old_curtab, *last_curtab;
    win_T	*new_curwin = NULL;
    tabpage_T	*new_curtab = NULL;
    int		prev_arglist_locked = arglist_locked;

#ifdef FEAT_CMDWIN
    if (cmdwin_type != 0)
    {
	emsg(_(e_invalid_in_cmdline_window));
	return;
    }
#endif
    if (ARGCOUNT <= 0)
    {
	// Don't give an error message.  We don't want it when the ":all"
	// command is in the .vimrc.
	return;
    }
    setpcmark();

    opened_len = ARGCOUNT;
    opened = alloc_clear(opened_len);
    if (opened == NULL)
	return;

    // Autocommands may do anything to the argument list.  Make sure it's not
    // freed while we are working here by "locking" it.  We still have to
    // watch out for its size to be changed.
    alist = curwin->w_alist;
    ++alist->al_refcount;
    arglist_locked = TRUE;

    old_curwin = curwin;
    old_curtab = curtab;

# ifdef FEAT_GUI
    need_mouse_correct = TRUE;
# endif

    // Try closing all windows that are not in the argument list.
    // Also close windows that are not full width;
    // When 'hidden' or "forceit" set the buffer becomes hidden.
    // Windows that have a changed buffer and can't be hidden won't be closed.
    // When the ":tab" modifier was used do this for all tab pages.
    if (had_tab > 0)
	goto_tabpage_tp(first_tabpage, TRUE, TRUE);
    for (;;)
    {
	tpnext = curtab->tp_next;
	for (wp = firstwin; wp != NULL; wp = wpnext)
	{
	    wpnext = wp->w_next;
	    buf = wp->w_buffer;
	    if (buf->b_ffname == NULL
		    || (!keep_tabs && (buf->b_nwindows > 1
			    || wp->w_width != Columns)))
		i = opened_len;
	    else
	    {
		// check if the buffer in this window is in the arglist
		for (i = 0; i < opened_len; ++i)
		{
		    if (i < alist->al_ga.ga_len
			    && (AARGLIST(alist)[i].ae_fnum == buf->b_fnum
				|| fullpathcmp(alist_name(&AARGLIST(alist)[i]),
					buf->b_ffname, TRUE, TRUE) & FPC_SAME))
		    {
			int weight = 1;

			if (old_curtab == curtab)
			{
			    ++weight;
			    if (old_curwin == wp)
				++weight;
			}

			if (weight > (int)opened[i])
			{
			    opened[i] = (char_u)weight;
			    if (i == 0)
			    {
				if (new_curwin != NULL)
				    new_curwin->w_arg_idx = opened_len;
				new_curwin = wp;
				new_curtab = curtab;
			    }
			}
			else if (keep_tabs)
			    i = opened_len;

			if (wp->w_alist != alist)
			{
			    // Use the current argument list for all windows
			    // containing a file from it.
			    alist_unlink(wp->w_alist);
			    wp->w_alist = alist;
			    ++wp->w_alist->al_refcount;
			}
			break;
		    }
		}
	    }
	    wp->w_arg_idx = i;

	    if (i == opened_len && !keep_tabs)// close this window
	    {
		if (buf_hide(buf) || forceit || buf->b_nwindows > 1
							|| !bufIsChanged(buf))
		{
		    // If the buffer was changed, and we would like to hide it,
		    // try autowriting.
		    if (!buf_hide(buf) && buf->b_nwindows <= 1
							 && bufIsChanged(buf))
		    {
			bufref_T    bufref;

			set_bufref(&bufref, buf);

			(void)autowrite(buf, FALSE);

			// check if autocommands removed the window
			if (!win_valid(wp) || !bufref_valid(&bufref))
			{
			    wpnext = firstwin;	// start all over...
			    continue;
			}
		    }
		    // don't close last window
		    if (ONE_WINDOW
			    && (first_tabpage->tp_next == NULL || !had_tab))
			use_firstwin = TRUE;
		    else
		    {
			win_close(wp, !buf_hide(buf) && !bufIsChanged(buf));

			// check if autocommands removed the next window
			if (!win_valid(wpnext))
			    wpnext = firstwin;	// start all over...
		    }
		}
	    }
	}

	// Without the ":tab" modifier only do the current tab page.
	if (had_tab == 0 || tpnext == NULL)
	    break;

	// check if autocommands removed the next tab page
	if (!valid_tabpage(tpnext))
	    tpnext = first_tabpage;	// start all over...

	goto_tabpage_tp(tpnext, TRUE, TRUE);
    }

    // Open a window for files in the argument list that don't have one.
    // ARGCOUNT may change while doing this, because of autocommands.
    if (count > opened_len || count <= 0)
	count = opened_len;

    // Don't execute Win/Buf Enter/Leave autocommands here.
    ++autocmd_no_enter;
    ++autocmd_no_leave;
    last_curwin = curwin;
    last_curtab = curtab;
    win_enter(lastwin, FALSE);
    // ":tab drop file" should re-use an empty window to avoid "--remote-tab"
    // leaving an empty tab page when executed locally.
    if (keep_tabs && BUFEMPTY() && curbuf->b_nwindows == 1
			    && curbuf->b_ffname == NULL && !curbuf->b_changed)
    {
	use_firstwin = TRUE;
	tab_drop_empty_window = TRUE;
    }

    for (i = 0; i < count && !got_int; ++i)
    {
	if (alist == &global_alist && i == global_alist.al_ga.ga_len - 1)
	    arg_had_last = TRUE;
	if (opened[i] > 0)
	{
	    // Move the already present window to below the current window
	    if (curwin->w_arg_idx != i)
	    {
		FOR_ALL_WINDOWS(wpnext)
		{
		    if (wpnext->w_arg_idx == i)
		    {
			if (keep_tabs)
			{
			    new_curwin = wpnext;
			    new_curtab = curtab;
			}
			else if (wpnext->w_frame->fr_parent
						 != curwin->w_frame->fr_parent)
			{
			    emsg(_("E249: window layout changed unexpectedly"));
			    i = count;
			    break;
			}
			else
			    win_move_after(wpnext, curwin);
			break;
		    }
		}
	    }
	}
	else if (split_ret == OK)
	{
	    // trigger events for tab drop
	    if (tab_drop_empty_window && i == count - 1)
		--autocmd_no_enter;
	    if (!use_firstwin)		// split current window
	    {
		p_ea_save = p_ea;
		p_ea = TRUE;		// use space from all windows
		split_ret = win_split(0, WSP_ROOM | WSP_BELOW);
		p_ea = p_ea_save;
		if (split_ret == FAIL)
		    continue;
	    }
	    else    // first window: do autocmd for leaving this buffer
		--autocmd_no_leave;

	    // edit file "i"
	    curwin->w_arg_idx = i;
	    if (i == 0)
	    {
		new_curwin = curwin;
		new_curtab = curtab;
	    }
	    (void)do_ecmd(0, alist_name(&AARGLIST(alist)[i]), NULL, NULL,
		      ECMD_ONE,
		      ((buf_hide(curwin->w_buffer)
			   || bufIsChanged(curwin->w_buffer)) ? ECMD_HIDE : 0)
						       + ECMD_OLDBUF, curwin);
	    if (tab_drop_empty_window && i == count - 1)
		++autocmd_no_enter;
	    if (use_firstwin)
		++autocmd_no_leave;
	    use_firstwin = FALSE;
	}
	ui_breakcheck();

	// When ":tab" was used open a new tab for a new window repeatedly.
	if (had_tab > 0 && tabpage_index(NULL) <= p_tpm)
	    cmdmod.cmod_tab = 9999;
    }

    // Remove the "lock" on the argument list.
    alist_unlink(alist);
    arglist_locked = prev_arglist_locked;

    --autocmd_no_enter;

    // restore last referenced tabpage's curwin
    if (last_curtab != new_curtab)
    {
	if (valid_tabpage(last_curtab))
	    goto_tabpage_tp(last_curtab, TRUE, TRUE);
	if (win_valid(last_curwin))
	    win_enter(last_curwin, FALSE);
    }
    // to window with first arg
    if (valid_tabpage(new_curtab))
	goto_tabpage_tp(new_curtab, TRUE, TRUE);
    if (win_valid(new_curwin))
	win_enter(new_curwin, FALSE);

    --autocmd_no_leave;
    vim_free(opened);
}

